From: kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie)
Subject:  Re: The George Washington U. Computer Ethics Policy
Message-ID: <CM09vH.FJA@cs.uiuc.edu>
Date: Tue, 1 Mar 1994 21:47:40 GMT

Summary: The policy seems to have been imposed without user
participation. It is so vague and broad that it prohibits all computer
use (an invitation to selective enforcement). It seems to treat
computer forums different from other university-owned forums,
prohibiting speech that that would be constitutionally protected at a
state university. It offers no due process protection.

>[From
>   gopher gwis.circ.gwu.edu 70
>    --> Computing Services
>        --> Computer Information and Resource Center (CIRC)
>            --> CIRC Computing Facilities (Opening hours, Location, etc.)
>                 --> The G.W.U. Ethics Policy
>]
>
>The GWU Computer Ethics Policy:
>
>     We express our appreciation to R. Brian Walsh of Notre Dame
>University Computer Center for our statement for ethics:

Notre Dame is a religious university, George Washington U. is a
private secular university. I would think the policy would need to be
modified a bit to reflect that difference.

>     It is considered a generally acceptable purpose among users to
>learn about the workings of the operating system.
[...]
>[...]the following, (but not limited to the following) is considered
>improper on our systems:
>
>     Frivolous use of computers.

This is so vague that people "of common intelligence must necessarily
guess at its meaning."

>     Inspection of data which have to do with computer
>     utilization, authorization for computer access, or security.

This is too broad. It prohibits 1) querying the system to see who is
currently logged in 2) querying the system to about one's own
"utilization, authorization, or security."

>     Interference with other users of the system.

This rule outlaws using a terminal if another user wishes to use it.

[...]
>     Sending obscene or vulgar messages.

What if the receiver *wants* to receive a vulgar message? What if the
message is sent to a public forum (like a newsgroup)? What is a
"vulgar message". Does the university ban all vulgarity or only
computer vulgarity? If it only censors computer vulgarity, why are
computers treated differently than other university-owned forums?

Most university student codes explicitly promise to protect the free
speech of students even in university forums. If GMU makes such a
promise then this rule violates university policy.

I suggest removing it and replacing it with a reference to the
university's general harassment policy.

[...]
>     University computing centers routinely cancel accounts. [...]
[...]

Are users given due process via the university-approved disciplinary
procedures? Can users request a hearing? Can they appeal? At most
universities, not even professors have authority to expel a student
from class. How did the computing center get authority to, on its own,
expel users from the computer system?

- Carl

