[A repost - Carl]

[paraphrase: [A user at csn.org:] "I sent a number of Mr. Whealton's
posts to his sysadmin [at the U. of Delaware].  His sysadmin, however,
does not feel that he has the right to revoke Mr. Whealton's
priviledges."]

Newsgroups: alt.homosexual
From: rawyatt@teal.csn.org (Robert A. Wyatt)
Subject:  Differentiating between CENSORSHIP and ABUSE OF PRIVILEDGES
Message-ID: <CM7ts0.25v@csn.org>
Date: Sat, 5 Mar 1994 23:40:47 GMT


I think it's time we started differentiating between censorship and the
abuse of priviledges.  I appreciate the posting of information from the 
EFF, but I can't say that I agree.

Universities and corporations who provide *free* access to their students
(or employees) have the right to make rules regarding Internet etiquette.
These schools/companies have paid for the equipment and they pay for the
services.  Therefore they *CAN* regularate how a person behaves on the
net *if* they choose to.

I sent a number of Mr. Whealton's posts to his sysadmin.  His sysadmin,
however, does not feel that he has the right to revoke Mr. Whealton's
priviledges.  In his opinion, Internet access is a right.  I can respect
this point of view.  Each institution takes a different approach.  Neither
is correct or incorrect.  

However, let's look at this whole situation another way.  You write an
article and submit it to Newsweek.  They don't like it, so they don't
print it.  Is this censorship?  No!  Newsweek has the right to decide what
it wants to print.  Now, you could go out and print up millions of
copies of your article and try to sell them.  No one will stop you.  The
same applies to Internet use.  You are using a machine you don't own and
someone else is paying the connect and administration costs.  If that 
person (or administration) finds your use of the system to be abusive,
they have the right to terminate your account.  You can always go out and
buy a computer and pay for an account through a commercial service
provider.  

We are confusing the issue of censorship with abuse of priviledges.  I
own my computer.  I pay for my account.  If I were running a business and
found someone slandering others on the net, I would revoke his/her
priviledges.  Call this censorship.  I call it my right as the owner
of the equipment.

And, finally, to those people who say "so, free speech is only for those
who can afford it"...Yes, in this case.  Free speech is going out on a
street-corner, printing up fliers, and paying for your own Internet
account.  If you happen to find a publication that wants to print your
article, that's great.  Just like finding a University or place of
employment that doesn't care what you say over the net.  However, just 
because your article didn't get published, that isn't censorship.  That's
a judegment call, a right reserved by the publication.  As is the right to
revoke an account when someone abuses the rules governing Internet 
priviledges.

-Rob
